ion.\n\nFEEDBACK\nStrong answer: Complete plan; counter answered, not ignored.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"A superficial change in length or appearance does not repair meaning. Listing reasons that repeat the same idea in different words. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example.","options":["Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example","A claim unrelated to the text","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no explanation"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about argument plan with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nPlanning an argument lists contention, 2–3 reasons with evidence types, a counterargument to answer and a reasoned order (strongest last or problem→solution). Now choose different people, facts or text to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake one fresh example demonstrating the skill and explain your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should meet the same purpose as the model, but contain original material.","explain":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy